{"id":"9d03e65e-b844-4965-903f-4aa451d3ba24","object":"chat.completion","created":1785037714,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(n['id'])\\n    roots = [nid for nid, n in node_map.items() if n.get('parent') is None]\\n    if len(roots) != 1:\\n        raise ValueError('Expected exactly one root')\\n    root_id = roots[0]\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols:\\n            symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym} not present in tree')\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if not n.get('removable', False):\\n            continue\\n        has_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_child:\\n            continue\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols:\\n            cnt = sum(1 for nd in node_map.values() if nd['symbol'] == sym and nd['id'] in retained)\\n            if cnt <= 1:\\n                continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for SE-PE-01. The task is grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree. The payload has nodes (array of objects with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols (list of strings). We need to produce removed_node_ids (list of strings) and retained_node_ids (list of strings) for the given case.\n\nThe objectives: reduce the tree by tentatively removing nodes if possible, preserving required symbols, and following a traversal order: deeper nodes before shallower nodes, and using node ID as tie-breaker at equal depth. The nodes have a parent field (null for root). The root is never removable. Some nodes have removable flag (true/false). We must not remove required symbols (symbols in required_symbols). Also we must ensure that after removal, the required symbols remain represented (i.e., each required symbol must still be present in some retained node? Actually \"Never remove a required symbol\" means if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, it cannot be removed. But also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests we can remove a node only if after removal, the set of symbols among retained nodes still covers all required_symbols. That implies we cannot remove a node whose symbol is required? Actually if the symbol is required, it itself is one of the required symbols. So removing it would make that symbol not represented. So it's automatically not allowed. But maybe there are multiple nodes with same symbol? The required_symbols are symbols, not node IDs. So we need to ensure that after removal, for each required symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains. So we can remove a node with a required symbol if there is another node with the same symbol retained. However, the spec says \"Never remove a required symbol\". That could be interpreted as never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But that would be too restrictive; in the example fixtures, required_symbols includes \"root\" and \"s1\". The root node has symbol \"root\" and is not removable anyway. There are multiple nodes with symbol \"s1\": n1 (depth1, removable false) and n4 (depth2, removable true). So if \"s1\" is required, we might be allowed to remove n4 because n1 still retains \"s1\". But the spec says \"Never remove a required symbol\" - ambiguous. Looking at the candidate artifact: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It explicitly says \"a required symbol\" meaning a node whose symbol is required? Or meaning the symbol itself? Usually in a tree reduction, a required symbol means that symbol must be present in the final tree. So removing a node with that symbol is allowed only if another node with same symbol remains. But the phrasing \"Never remove a required symbol\" suggests that if a node's symbol is required, it should not be removed. However, then the later condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" would be redundant because if you never remove a required symbol node, then all required symbols remain represented automatically. But maybe the required_symbols are about semantic requirement; a node with that symbol is needed, but you can remove it if its functionality can be replaced? The candidate says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes. ... Never remove ... a required symbol ... Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies you might remove a node with a required symbol if there is another node with the same symbol that retains it. So I think the intention is: a node can be removed if its 'removable' flag is true, and it's not the root, and it has no retained children (i.e., all its descendants have been removed? Or it has no children that are retained? The spec says \"a node with retained children\" should not be removed. So we can only remove leaf nodes (nodes with no children that are retained at that moment). Also we need to ensure that after removal, the required symbols set is still covered. That means if the node's symbol is required, we need at least one other node with that symbol retained.\n\nAlso, we need to process nodes in order: deeper first, then by node ID tie-breaker. So we should sort all nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending (string compare? Node IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. Typically lexicographic order is fine). For each node in that order, if it is removable (and not root, and not required? Actually we will check conditions), and if it has no retained children (children that are still in the retained set), and if removing it would leave all required symbols still represented (i.e., after removing it, for each required symbol, there exists at least one retained node with that symbol), then we remove it. We must maintain a set of retained node IDs initially all nodes. Then as we remove nodes, update retained set. The removed_node_ids list will be the order of removal (the order we tentatively remove). The retained_node_ids after all removals should be sorted alphabetically? The spec says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (ascending, presumably string sort). removed_node_ids should be in removal order (the order we processed and removed them).\n\nAdditionally, we need to consider grammar validity? The candidate mentions \"grammar_valid\", but the case payload does not provide grammar rules. It only has symbols and parent-child relationships. The task is \"grammar-guided node reduction\", but the only constraints given are parent-child and required symbols. Possibly the grammar validity is implicit: the tree is already grammar-valid. The reduction must maintain grammar validity? Actually the spec says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" and \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" But we don't have grammar rules. Perhaps the tree structure itself is grammar-valid by definition; we just keep it as a subtree (since we only remove nodes, the remaining nodes still form a valid tree? Not necessarily: if we remove a node, its children become orphans? The spec doesn't mention re-parenting. Typically in parse tree reduction, if you remove a node, its children are also removed (since they are dependent). But the spec says \"a node with retained children\" should not be removed. That implies if a node has children that are retained, you cannot remove it. So we only remove leaf nodes. That ensures the tree structure remains connected because the parent of a removed leaf is still there. There's no mention of re-parenting children to the removed node's parent. So we assume we only remove nodes that have no children that are still retained. This is consistent with the depth-first reduction.\n\nAlso, we need to consider the concept of \"registered_candidate_order\" and \"stable_ties\". That suggests we process nodes in a deterministic order. Deeper first, then ID tie-break.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Parse case payload: nodes list, required_symbols list.\n2. Build a mapping from node ID to node info.\n3. Also build a mapping from parent ID to list of children IDs.\n4. Initialize retained_ids set with all node IDs.\n5. Determine root (parent null). Root is not removable (its removable flag is false).\n6. Filter nodes that are removable? Actually we will consider all nodes except root, and only removable flag true. However, we might also consider nodes that have removable false but we can still attempt? The spec says \"removable\" is a flag; only nodes with removable true can be removed. So we only attempt removal for nodes with removable==true.\n7. Sort nodes by (depth descending, id ascending). The IDs are strings, so we can use natural string sort (like \"n1\", \"n2\", \"n10\"? But these IDs are like \"n1\", \"n2\", etc. They are small numbers; string sort is fine. Or we can extract numeric part? Better to use string comparison as tiebreaker.\n8. For each node in that order:\n   - If node.id not in retained_ids: skip (already removed).\n   - If node.id is root (parent null), skip.\n   - If node.removable is False, skip.\n   - Check if node has any children that are still retained. We have children map; iterate over children and check if child id in retained_ids. If any, skip (cannot remove because it has retained children).\n   - Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to become missing. For each required symbol, check if there is at least one retained node (including possibly this node) with that symbol. If we remove this node, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, after removal, there remains at least one node with that symbol. So we can precompute the count of each required symbol among retained nodes. Then for the node being considered, if its symbol is required, we check if count of that symbol > 1 (i.e., at least one other node has that symbol). If count == 1 and this node is the only one, then we cannot remove. We can update counts as we remove nodes.\n   - If all conditions satisfied, we mark node for removal: remove id from retained_ids, append id to removed_node_ids list, and update counts: decrement count for its symbol (if symbol is required).\n9. After processing all nodes, we have removed_node_ids (in removal order) and retained_node_ids (sorted ascending).\n\nEdge cases: What if there are multiple nodes with same symbol, and the required symbol is present but only on removable nodes? We need to ensure after removal, at least one remains. Our count check handles that.\n\nNote: The initial set of retained nodes is all nodes. The root is always retained (non-removable). Required symbols must be present in retained nodes; the root has symbol \"root\" which is always required. So no problem.\n\nNow, about \"sorted retained IDs\": We need to output a list of strings sorted. We'll sort alphabetically.\n\nAlso, we must not mutate the input case. We'll make copies.\n\nNow, implement solve(case) as per the requirements.\n\nThe output must be a dictionary with keys \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\". Both arrays of strings.\n\nWe'll raise ValueError for infeasible? For example, if after removal, a required symbol is missing? But we only remove if condition holds, so it should be feasible. However, if the initial tree does not contain all required symbols, that's infeasible. But the fixtures guarantee they do. We'll still validate: for each required symbol, there must be at least one node with that symbol initially. If not, raise ValueError. Also, if root is missing or has parent non-null, but not needed.\n\nNow, write the Python code.\n\nWe'll include comments? Not needed but fine.\n\nNow, produce the response.\n\nWe need to format as JSON with key \"implementation\" containing the string of the Python source code, properly escaped.\n\nWe'll generate the code.\n\nLet's write solve function:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    \n    # Build dicts\n    node_map = {}\n    children_map = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        node_map[nid] = n\n        parent = n.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n        # handle root parent None\n\n    # Check root\n    roots = [nid for nid, n in node_map.items() if n.get('parent') is None]\n    if len(roots) != 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected exactly one root\")\n    root_id = roots[0]\n\n    # Verify all required symbols present\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        if sym in required_symbols:\n            symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {sym} not present in tree\")\n\n    # Initially all nodes retained\n    retained_ids = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed_node_ids = []\n\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n\n    # For each node in order\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid not in retained_ids:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        if not n.get('removable', False):\n            continue\n        # Check children: if any child retained, skip\n        has_retained_child = False\n        for child_id in children_map.get(nid, []):\n            if child_id in retained_ids:\n                has_retained_child = True\n                break\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols coverage after removal\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        can_remove = True\n        if sym in required_symbols:\n            # Count how many retained nodes have this symbol (including this node)\n            count = sum(1 for n2 in node_map.values() if n2['symbol'] == sym and n2['id'] in retained_ids)\n            if count <= 1:\n                can_remove = False\n        if can_remove:\n            retained_ids.remove(nid)\n            removed_node_ids.append(nid)\n            # Update symbol counts if needed: already handled by count check each time\n            # but for efficiency we could update, but not necessary\n\n    # After removals, retained_ids sorted\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained_ids)\n\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids\n    }\n\nWe need to ensure we don't have any dependencies beyond standard library. The code uses basic data structures.\n\nNow, produce the JSON response. We'll enclose the code as a string, escaping newlines and quotes.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":3511,"total_tokens":5683,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3028},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}